Graduate Student Government\nProfessional Development\nResources for Postdocs\nOLLI Scholars Program\nResearch & Innovation\nGraduate Student Travel Award\nSummer Research Opportunities for Undergrads\nUD Research Office\nGraduate Student Seminar Series\nSpark! Symposium\nFrontier Fellows\nMentoring\nNews & Events\nGraduate College News\nGraduate College Newsletter\nEvents / Calendar\nArchived Events\nCOVID Updates\nGraduate College Event Photography and Videography\nGraduate College Contest Guidelines\nAlumni & Friends\nGive\nUD Home\nGraduate College\nProspective Students\nGraduate Programs\nBiological Sciences\nBiological Sciences\nBiological Sciences: MS, PhD, Non-Thesis Biology MS, BS/MS 4+1, BA/MS 4+1\nApply Today\n>\nInquire Today\n>\nProgram Website\n>\nAbout this Program\nThe Department of Biological Sciences offers Master of Science and Doctor of Philosophy degrees with focus on cell and developmental biology, neurobiology, microbiology, physiology, molecular biology, and genetics. Formalized concentrations provide students with focused research training and individualized career mentorship. All research PhD students receive financial support through assistantships. PhD graduate assistants in Biological Sciences also receive a full tuition scholarship from the University of Delaware and university-subsidized health insurance.\nThe Department of Biological Sciences also offers a non-thesis Biology MS, along with accelerated Biological Sciences BS/Biology MS 4+1 and Biological Sciences BA/Biology MS 4+1 programs. These programs are designed to strengthen the knowledge, skills, and competencies needed for careers in biotechnology and related industries or for advanced professional education in the clinical and medical sciences, including medical, dental, and physician assistant programs. The Biology MS and 4+1 programs each offer two concentrations tailored to students pursuing careers in biomedical sciences or in biotechnology and related industries. The Biology MS requires a minimum of 30 credits and is designed to be completed in one year of full-time study. The 4+1 programs provide an accelerated pathway to an MS in Biology, allowing students to complete their combined Bachelor's and Master's studies within five years of full-time enrollment before entering the workforce or pursuing additional professional education. Students may also develop technical competencies for laboratory-based careers in the life sciences through internships and practical academic laboratory experiences relevant to industry, academic, or government settings.\nProgram Concentrations:\nCell and Organ Systems Physiology Concentration (M.S., Ph.D.)\nChemistry-Biology Interface Concentration (Ph.D.)\nMolecular Biology and Genetics Concentration (M.S., Ph.D.)\nBiomedical Studies (non-thesis M.S.; 4+1)\nBiotechnical Applications (non-thesis M.S.; 4+1)\nDegrees Offered:\nBiological Sciences \u2013 M.S.\nBiological Sciences - Ph.D.\nBiological Sciences - M.S. (non-thesis)\nBiological Sciences \u2013 B.A./Biology \u2013 M.S. (Biomedical Studies or Biotechnical Applications concentrations), (4+1)\nBiological Sciences \u2013 B.S./Biology \u2013 M.S., (Biomedical Studies or Biotechnical Applications concentrations), (4+1)\nBiological Sciences \u2013 B.A./Secondary STEM Education \u2013 M.S. (4+1)\nBiological Sciences \u2013 B.S./Secondary STEM Education \u2013 M.S. (4+1)\nApplication Deadlines\nFALL:\nDecember 15: Priority consideration for admission\nApril 15: Final deadline to apply\nUD Tuition\nThe 2026-2027 UD graduate student tuition rate per credit hour is $1,149.\nAll or nearly all students in the thesis-based M.S. and Ph.D. programs receive a stipend and full tuition scholarship.\nContact Info\nDepartment of Biological Sciences\nDepartment\nHeather Justison\nProgram Contact\nErica Selva\nProgram Co-Director\nJessica Tanis\nProgram Co-Director\nVelia M. Fowler\nDepartment Chair\nProgram Links\nProgram Website\nProgram Admission Requirements\nProg